[{:db/id 17592186105826 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c1276b85-cb47-56f4-9380-ccdb52fdaba5" "question-02d7c8e8-c1c1-5349-b786-2cc03d634147"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c1276b85-cb47-56f4-9380-ccdb52fdaba5" :question/id #uuid "c1276b85-cb47-56f4-9380-ccdb52fdaba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-89400" :question/problem "In a coffee shop, $28%$ of the customers order their coffee to go. If $12$ customers from the coffee shop are randomly selected, what is the probability that $3$ or less will order their coffee to go? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of customers that order their coffee to go.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ customers\n\n- the probability that each customer orders the coffee to go is $p=28%=0.28$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.28).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(0.28)}^{x}{(1-0.28)}^{12-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(0.28)}^{x}{(0.72)}^{12-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., the customer orders the coffee to go) on at most $3$ trials. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0}){(0.28)}^{0}{(0.72)}^{12}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1}){(0.28)}^{1}{(0.72)}^{11}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{2}){(0.28)}^{2}{(0.72)}^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{3}){(0.28)}^{3}{(0.72)}^{9} \\\\  & ={(0.28)}^{0}{(0.72)}^{12}+12{(0.28)}^{1}{(0.72)}^{11}+66{(0.28)}^{2}{(0.72)}^{10}+220{(0.28)}^{3}{(0.72)}^{9} \\\\  & =0.555\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27c18810-179b-53f0-a245-19fdca7de9b4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27c18810-179b-53f0-a245-19fdca7de9b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "27c18810-179b-53f0-a245-19fdca7de9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-90044cbf-45a0-57af-832e-49250df1a7d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-318f6f4d-16fa-51e6-b179-78e91115f020" "answer-f5b00b74-2769-5031-9b6b-ad1e823bdbaa" "answer-2ebbeff3-c5ba-5410-a408-4b1fcdd81dd9" "answer-87d0f7be-dc1b-5137-82d6-ad06ca042afd" "answer-90044cbf-45a0-57af-832e-49250df1a7d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-318f6f4d-16fa-51e6-b179-78e91115f020" :answer/id #uuid "318f6f4d-16fa-51e6-b179-78e91115f020" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.254" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5b00b74-2769-5031-9b6b-ad1e823bdbaa" :answer/id #uuid "f5b00b74-2769-5031-9b6b-ad1e823bdbaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.444" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ebbeff3-c5ba-5410-a408-4b1fcdd81dd9" :answer/id #uuid "2ebbeff3-c5ba-5410-a408-4b1fcdd81dd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.452" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87d0f7be-dc1b-5137-82d6-ad06ca042afd" :answer/id #uuid "87d0f7be-dc1b-5137-82d6-ad06ca042afd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.524" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90044cbf-45a0-57af-832e-49250df1a7d0" :answer/id #uuid "90044cbf-45a0-57af-832e-49250df1a7d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.555" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105830 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4aa4655e-20ee-52b5-8d5f-be71eb721eca" "question-a1fbc871-5104-5d0c-b476-cd8414e81086"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4aa4655e-20ee-52b5-8d5f-be71eb721eca" :question/id #uuid "4aa4655e-20ee-52b5-8d5f-be71eb721eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-89402" :question/problem "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $20$ times. If the archer has a $20%$ chance of hitting the bullseye, what is the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the arrow hits the bullseye. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ trials\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye is $p=20%=0.2$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.2).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(1-0.2)^{20-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{20-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=20)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{20-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{20-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.8)^{20}+20(0.2)(0.8)^{19}] \\\\  & =1-0.069\\,175 \\\\  & =0.931\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d0f991d-9f23-5d9c-9970-8ed3243602c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d0f991d-9f23-5d9c-9970-8ed3243602c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0f991d-9f23-5d9c-9970-8ed3243602c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-69c9acef-9d33-557c-bd83-45aa9da4aecf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b7b21599-df3a-56c7-be2f-08eed20c971f" "answer-22f0b3b1-6694-51fa-b8a9-943b0a524d0b" "answer-c283cf27-9162-56a8-84b2-ce532ab362b9" "answer-69c9acef-9d33-557c-bd83-45aa9da4aecf" "answer-f90a7844-0f0f-53a0-969b-4a261303ee14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7b21599-df3a-56c7-be2f-08eed20c971f" :answer/id #uuid "b7b21599-df3a-56c7-be2f-08eed20c971f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.738" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22f0b3b1-6694-51fa-b8a9-943b0a524d0b" :answer/id #uuid "22f0b3b1-6694-51fa-b8a9-943b0a524d0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.871" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c283cf27-9162-56a8-84b2-ce532ab362b9" :answer/id #uuid "c283cf27-9162-56a8-84b2-ce532ab362b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.893" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69c9acef-9d33-557c-bd83-45aa9da4aecf" :answer/id #uuid "69c9acef-9d33-557c-bd83-45aa9da4aecf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.931" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f90a7844-0f0f-53a0-969b-4a261303ee14" :answer/id #uuid "f90a7844-0f0f-53a0-969b-4a261303ee14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.973" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a1fbc871-5104-5d0c-b476-cd8414e81086" :question/id #uuid "a1fbc871-5104-5d0c-b476-cd8414e81086" :question/math-academy-id "q-276107" :question/problem "An unfair coin has a $15%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $10$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=15%=0.15.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.15).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(1-0.15)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(0.85)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.15)^{0}(0.85)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.15)^{1}(0.85)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.85)^{10}+10(0.15)(0.85)^{9}] \\\\  & ≈1-0.544\\,300 \\\\  & ≈0.456,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aeab778f-c7ec-5e9d-a3d3-497eb92a9d85"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aeab778f-c7ec-5e9d-a3d3-497eb92a9d85" :answer-field/id #uuid "aeab778f-c7ec-5e9d-a3d3-497eb92a9d85" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-66e78e4d-d3e6-5a87-a64a-a52faa941ce7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-66e78e4d-d3e6-5a87-a64a-a52faa941ce7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66e78e4d-d3e6-5a87-a64a-a52faa941ce7" :answer/id #uuid "66e78e4d-d3e6-5a87-a64a-a52faa941ce7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.456" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f8e22e39-9704-57eb-a912-cb7e8741cbdb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f8e22e39-9704-57eb-a912-cb7e8741cbdb" :question/id #uuid "f8e22e39-9704-57eb-a912-cb7e8741cbdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-331490" :question/problem "A fair coin is flipped $4$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads exactly $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin lands on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=4$ flips.\n\n- The probability of getting heads on a single flip is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=2$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =0.375,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8fee46f-9f97-52a3-886a-81612f3c88c5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8fee46f-9f97-52a3-886a-81612f3c88c5" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8fee46f-9f97-52a3-886a-81612f3c88c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8b365ee9-c153-5ddd-a40d-da2203634354" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b365ee9-c153-5ddd-a40d-da2203634354"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b365ee9-c153-5ddd-a40d-da2203634354" :answer/id #uuid "8b365ee9-c153-5ddd-a40d-da2203634354" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.375" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-02d7c8e8-c1c1-5349-b786-2cc03d634147" :question/id #uuid "02d7c8e8-c1c1-5349-b786-2cc03d634147" :question/math-academy-id "q-331528" :question/problem "A quality‑check sensor detects a flaw with probability $40%.$ If $5$ items are inspected, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at most $2$ of them contain a flaw?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of flawed items detected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ items.\n\n- The probability of a flaw is $p=40%=0.4.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.4).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $2$ of the $5$ items contain flaws. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & =1(0.6)^{5}+5(0.4)(0.6)^{4}+10(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.683,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d40b2fb2-406e-5639-97e3-0ab4b3c53608"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d40b2fb2-406e-5639-97e3-0ab4b3c53608" :answer-field/id #uuid "d40b2fb2-406e-5639-97e3-0ab4b3c53608" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fca83564-ebe2-59c0-a75f-f0b7a4ff3ff4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fca83564-ebe2-59c0-a75f-f0b7a4ff3ff4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fca83564-ebe2-59c0-a75f-f0b7a4ff3ff4" :answer/id #uuid "fca83564-ebe2-59c0-a75f-f0b7a4ff3ff4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.683" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-72ce6bb1-8990-56c4-ba78-cb52ead69a49" :activity/id #uuid "72ce6bb1-8990-56c4-ba78-cb52ead69a49" :activity/title "Modeling With the Binomial Distribution" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049632 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-4c7aaf17-b2f0-56e9-b409-e8d31a1d7256" "step-e43d6552-8e48-5f1e-9be0-84d2d3b26aca" "step-07a22149-edba-534b-8341-9087e596b91c" "step-a30dec52-53b5-50a0-bd69-3bd1b30e2334" "step-b3a85073-43c3-5155-b787-157bb738d375"] :activity/first-step "step-4c7aaf17-b2f0-56e9-b409-e8d31a1d7256" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-4c7aaf17-b2f0-56e9-b409-e8d31a1d7256" :step/id #uuid "4c7aaf17-b2f0-56e9-b409-e8d31a1d7256" :step/content "question-c1276b85-cb47-56f4-9380-ccdb52fdaba5" :step/next "step-e43d6552-8e48-5f1e-9be0-84d2d3b26aca" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e43d6552-8e48-5f1e-9be0-84d2d3b26aca" :step/id #uuid "e43d6552-8e48-5f1e-9be0-84d2d3b26aca" :step/content "question-4aa4655e-20ee-52b5-8d5f-be71eb721eca" :step/next "step-07a22149-edba-534b-8341-9087e596b91c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-07a22149-edba-534b-8341-9087e596b91c" :step/id #uuid "07a22149-edba-534b-8341-9087e596b91c" :step/content "question-a1fbc871-5104-5d0c-b476-cd8414e81086" :step/next "step-a30dec52-53b5-50a0-bd69-3bd1b30e2334" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a30dec52-53b5-50a0-bd69-3bd1b30e2334" :step/id #uuid "a30dec52-53b5-50a0-bd69-3bd1b30e2334" :step/content "question-f8e22e39-9704-57eb-a912-cb7e8741cbdb" :step/next "step-b3a85073-43c3-5155-b787-157bb738d375" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b3a85073-43c3-5155-b787-157bb738d375" :step/id #uuid "b3a85073-43c3-5155-b787-157bb738d375" :step/content "question-02d7c8e8-c1c1-5349-b786-2cc03d634147" :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
